  "source": "one-continuous-master",
  "lines": [
    {
      "id": "line-01",
      "text": "Pinocchio carried Geppetto through the sea until his strength began failing.",
      "start": 0.107,
      "end": 5.487
    },
    {
      "id": "line-02",
      "text": "The friendly Tunny arrived and carried both exhausted travelers safely ashore.",
      "start": 5.487,
      "end": 11.503
    },
    {
      "id": "line-03",
      "text": "On the road, the ruined Fox and Cat begged their former victim for money.",
      "start": 11.503,
      "end": 17.977
    },
    {
      "id": "line-04",
      "text": "Pinocchio remembered their theft, rejected their excuses, and continued with his father.",
      "start": 17.977,
      "end": 24.784
    },
    {
      "id": "line-05",
      "text": "The Talking Cricket welcomed them into a small straw cottage.",
      "start": 24.784,
      "end": 30.871
    },
    {
      "id": "line-06",
      "text": "Pinocchio apologized for the hammer and prepared a bed for Geppetto.",
      "start": 30.871,
      "end": 35.886
    },
    {
      "id": "line-07",
      "text": "Farmer John offered warm milk in exchange for one hundred buckets of water.",
      "start": 35.886,
      "end": 41.807
    },
    {
      "id": "line-08",
      "text": "Every morning, Pinocchio completed the hard work to feed his weak father.",
      "start": 41.807,
      "end": 48.23
    },
    {
      "id": "line-09",
      "text": "At the farm, he discovered Lamp-Wick dying after a donkey's life of exhaustion.",
      "start": 48.23,
      "end": 54.721
    },
    {
      "id": "line-10",
      "text": "For five months, Pinocchio worked by day and studied by lamplight.",
      "start": 54.721,
      "end": 60.674
    },
    {
      "id": "line-11",
      "text": "He sold baskets, supported Geppetto, and built him a comfortable rolling chair.",
      "start": 60.674,
      "end": 66.823
    },
    {
      "id": "line-12",
      "text": "Eventually he saved fifty pennies and planned to buy new clothes.",
      "start": 66.823,
      "end": 72.793
    },
    {
      "id": "line-13",
      "text": "The Snail then reported that the Blue Fairy was sick and penniless.",
      "start": 72.793,
      "end": 78.311
    },
    {
      "id": "line-14",
      "text": "Pinocchio gave away every coin and doubled his work for her.",
      "start": 78.311,
      "end": 84.202
    },
    {
      "id": "line-15",
      "text": "That night, the Fairy praised his loving heart and forgave his past.",
      "start": 84.202,
      "end": 90.041
    },
    {
      "id": "line-16",
      "text": "He awoke as a real boy in a renewed home beside a healthy Geppetto.",
      "start": 90.041,
      "end": 96.48
    }
  ],
